  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 135 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nAs stated in the Short Term discussion, current water vapor imagery \nand latest upper air analysis shows the upper-level ridge axis \nextending from the Missouri River Valley to the Ark-La-Tex region. \nMeanwhile, an upper-level low associated with a trough is located \nover the Pacific Northwest. Medium to long-range models show the \nridge shifting a little farther east each day as the upper-low \nejects into the Northern Plains. This is consistent with \nexpectations as height rises are observed in the southeastern CONUS \nand height falls ahead of the trough (in addition to a strong \nwesterly 50-55 kt jet at its base). Despite the eastward \ndisplacement of the ridge, our forecast area remains on its far \nwestern fringes. This allows for continued above normal temperatures \nand a gradual cool-down by a degree or two each day this week. \nIncreasing moisture in addition to upslope flow and disturbances \nrounding the western edge of the ridge yields daily chances \n(generally 20-50%, up to 60%) of showers/storms across the western \nhigher terrain and southeast New Mexico. \n\nLong-range model guidance is trying to signal a break in the \nweather pattern by next weekend, bringing a broad upper trough into \nour region. This would mean cooler temperatures and possibly more \nwidespread rain chances. However, there is significant disagreement \nin how far south the trough digs and the placement of a cold front. \nEnsemble box and whisker plots depict this with large spread in \ntemperature and precipitation fields, providing us with low \nconfidence in specific details at this time. \n  \nGreening",
